WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.010
Right now you are certain you are listening to this.

2
00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:08.580
Last night, inside your dream, you were exactly this certain that everything was real.

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:15.910
From the inside, how do you tell the two apart — and if you cannot, what are we actually claiming when we say "I know"?

4
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.280
Gecenin bir yerinde uyanıyorsunuz.

5
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.290
Somewhere in the night, you wake up.

6
00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:35.220
The room is dark, the blanket heavy, your toes are cold.

7
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.350
You roll over and check your phone: it is a little past 3.

8
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:48.600
A moment ago you were somewhere else entirely — walking down the hallway of a house you had never seen, or explaining something to someone you had not laid eyes on in years.

9
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.770
Now you are awake, and you know it.

10
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.100
You know it without a flicker of hesitation.

11
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.310
But on what grounds?

12
00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.760
Ask yourself: what exactly did you do to reach that verdict?

13
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.050
Did you test the texture of the sheets, probe the reality of the light?

14
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.780
No.

15
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.670
You did nothing at all.

16
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.320
You knew you were awake from the sheer fact of being awake.

17
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:24.170
That is a circular answer, and this is where the discomfort begins — because half an hour earlier, walking down the hallway of that unfamiliar house, you did not doubt for a single instant that you were there.

18
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.300
The strangest thing about a dream is not its content.

19
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.350
It is its certainty.

20
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:38.960
In dreams, people fly, dead relatives drink tea in the kitchen, you find your own house in a city you have never visited — and you do not object.

21
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.210
The scene you will call absurd in the morning contained nothing absurd at all while it was running.

22
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.180
What is more, you can dream that you have woken up.

23
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:58.710
It is called a false awakening: you see yourself getting out of bed, opening the curtains, even writing your dream down in a notebook — and then you wake up for real and find the page blank.

24
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.000
Some people go through this several times in a row.

25
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.530
Dream laboratories have gathered one consistent detail over the years: reading is hard in dreams.

26
00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:17.660
You look at a sign, a page of a book, a phone screen — the letters will not hold still, the words change on second glance.

27
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.990
A digital clock displays something nonsensical.

28
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.000
The face in the mirror is not quite your face.

29
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.410
All of it is right there, out in the open, and the person in the dream notices none of it.

30
00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.580
And there is the knot of the problem.

31
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:40.710
A dream does not fool you because it is a good illusion; it fools you despite being a bad one, because there is nobody inside it to raise a doubt.

32
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.360
Brain imaging gives us a solid clue as to why.

33
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:53.610
In REM sleep, where dreams cluster, the visual and emotional regions run almost as hard as they do in waking life — sometimes harder.

34
00:02:53.610 --> 00:03:01.740
The frontal regions behind the forehead, meanwhile, the ones that handle comparison, planning and self-monitoring, fall largely silent.

35
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:09.230
So in a dream there is a scene, there is music, there is feeling; what is missing is the editor, the one who says hold on a second.

36
00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.400
The organ that audits what you see is asleep.

37
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.770
But not always.

38
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:21.300
Some people, right in the middle of a dream, while the dream is still running, realize that it is a dream.

39
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:34.550
It is called lucid dreaming, and we have measurements of how common it is: a review published in 2016, pooling 50 years of research, reports that 55 percent of people have experienced it at least once in their lives,

40
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:39.800
and that the share who experience it weekly or more often sits at around 23 percent.

41
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.850
Imaging studies show that at the moment of lucidity, some of those silenced frontal regions come back online.

42
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.820
The auditor wakes up; the scene carries on.

43
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:55.070
For a long time this was a story people told, not something anyone had proved.

44
00:03:55.070 --> 00:04:01.760
Because when someone says that in their dream they realized they were dreaming, all you have is their report, delivered in the morning.

45
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:09.090
In 1975, in Britain, a researcher named Keith Hearne tried something simple to get past that.

46
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:18.820
He made an arrangement with his volunteer, Alan Worsley: if you become lucid inside the dream, move your eyes left and right in the pattern we agreed on beforehand.

47
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:26.470
Eye movements still reach the muscles during sleep, because the eyes are one of the few places spared from REM paralysis.

48
00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:33.960
That night, in the middle of deep REM sleep, the pen of the recording device traced the agreed-upon signal.

49
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.250
A sleeping human being had sent a message to the outside world.

50
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:51.020
Hearne published his finding in an obscure journal and it went largely unheard; the work was taken seriously only when Stephen LaBerge, who developed the same method independently, published his Stanford study in 1981.

51
00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.350
What came next is more startling still.

52
00:04:54.350 --> 00:05:06.480
In 2021, four separate laboratories in four countries published a joint experiment: with 36 participants, during REM sleep confirmed by sleep recordings, they attempted two-way communication.

53
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:13.970
The sleeping person was asked a question out loud — a simple subtraction, say, 8 minus 6.

54
00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:19.220
The answer came back through eye movements or through selected contractions of the facial muscles.

55
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.950
From 6 participants, a total of 29 correct answers were recorded.

56
00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:30.840
Asking a sleeping human a question and getting the right answer is now something that can actually be done.

57
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.770
So waking and sleeping are not two sealed rooms with no door between them.

58
00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.460
It is something that can be noticed.

59
00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.710
And if it can be noticed, it is a skill; and if it is a skill, it can fail.

60
00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.840
Which leaves exactly one question: by what test?

61
00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:57.090
There are two men who pushed that question all the way to the end — one in Baghdad, and one in the Netherlands, sitting beside a stove.

62
00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:09.700
In 1641, Descartes published the Meditations on First Philosophy, and it opens with something almost without precedent in the history of philosophy: the author resolves to bring down everything he knows,

63
00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:13.190
not piece by piece, but all at once.

64
00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:19.080
Dealing with beliefs one at a time takes too long; the clever move is to demolish the foundation.

65
00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:25.450
A stick in water looks bent; a distant tower looks round, and then turns out to be square as you approach.

66
00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:29.420
The senses deceive — this much was already known.

67
00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:39.150
But in the Meditations he climbs one step higher: right now, sitting by the stove, in my dressing gown, this paper in my hand — how could I doubt that?

68
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:43.760
The answer: I have dreamt exactly this, in exactly this much detail.

69
00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.530
I can find no certain sign that separates waking from dreaming.

70
00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.540
Descartes does not stop there.

71
00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.070
What about mathematics?

72
00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:00.440
In a dream, too, 2 and 3 make 5; in a dream, too, a square has four sides.

73
00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:05.370
Shapes, numbers, the simplest chains of reasoning seem immune to illusion.

74
00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:15.900
And it is at that point that the coldest idea in the text arrives: suppose there is a being of immense power and immense cunning, who has devoted all of that power to deceiving me.

75
00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.270
The evil demon.

76
00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:24.480
Then it is not only what I see that belongs to him, but that very feeling of certainty I have while counting.

77
00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.610
It could be a confidence planted in me every time I add 2 and 3.

78
00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:32.900
And so the last solid ground beneath my feet gives way as well.

79
00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:41.350
Descartes did not do this in order to destroy knowledge; he was looking for one single point that would not collapse, and he found it.

80
00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:45.800
However much I am being deceived, there is someone being deceived.

81
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.290
I think, therefore I am.

82
00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:55.660
Out of all that demolition, one thing came through alive: the existence of someone in the act of thinking.

83
00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:04.590
Along a path remarkably close to this line of reasoning, 5 and a half centuries before Descartes, someone else had walked — inside an utterly different life.

84
00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:17.040
Al-Ghazali was born in 1058 in the city of Tus, in Khorasan, and by his early thirties he was heading the Nizamiyya madrasa in Baghdad, the most prestigious chair in the Islamic world of his day.

85
00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.130
Hundreds of students, the prestige of the state, an authority beyond dispute.

86
00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:30.580
At this point in his life, by his own account, the question of the certainty of knowledge began to break him down from the inside.

87
00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:41.110
In the small autobiography he wrote in his mature years, al-Munqidh min al-dalal, that is, The Deliverance from Error, this is what he describes: he was searching for certain knowledge,

88
00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.280
knowledge that no doubt could gnaw at.

89
00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.450
First he saw that he could not trust the senses.

90
00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:51.580
Then he turned to reason, but there too a voice rose up.

91
00:08:51.580 --> 00:09:02.430
The most unsettling passage in The Deliverance from Error runs like this: while asleep, you believe the things you see are certain; when you wake, you realize none of them corresponded to anything.

92
00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:10.720
So who guarantees to you the reliability of what you now receive, awake, from the senses and from reason?

93
00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:17.090
Perhaps there is a state beyond waking, and waking stands to it exactly as dreaming stands to waking.

94
00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:28.580
Al-Ghazali ties this possibility to the states the Sufis describe and to what comes after death; he recalls a saying attributed to the Prophet: people are asleep, and when they die they wake.

95
00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:33.350
For al-Ghazali, this period did not stay a mere exercise on the page.

96
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:42.440
In his own words, the condition lasted about 2 months, and throughout that time he was, not in word or in claim but in fact, a complete skeptic.

97
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:48.810
And what ended it was not a proof either; by his account, it was a light that fell into his heart.

98
00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:55.340
Years later, in the middle of 1095, that same upheaval found an answer in his body this time.

99
00:09:55.340 --> 00:10:05.390
For something like 6 months he was torn between two sides: on the one hand the chair, the standing, the salary; on the other, the sense that none of it rested on anything.

100
00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:08.240
In the end his tongue froze.

101
00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:13.330
He could not speak; he would open his mouth to teach, and no sound would come.

102
00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:17.300
Physicians examined him and said the cause was not in the body.

103
00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:28.950
After those silent months he left Baghdad, the chair and his fame behind; for roughly 11 years he travelled to Damascus, to Jerusalem, to Mecca, and for a long time he did not return to teaching.

104
00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:33.400
Two men came to the edge of the same abyss and did different things.

105
00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.730
Descartes tried to build a bridge of proof across it.

106
00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:44.060
Al-Ghazali concluded that no such bridge could be built: to his mind, doubt could not be defeated with doubt's own weapon.

107
00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.750
Neither of them was playing a game.

108
00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:50.400
And neither of them was the first to ask the question.

109
00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:59.490
About 2 thousand years before Descartes, in the fourth century before Christ, a thinker in China named Zhuangzi fitted the same question into a few lines.

110
00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:03.940
Here is what he tells us: in his dream he had become a butterfly.

111
00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:08.870
Wings beating, delighted to be flying, a butterfly through and through.

112
00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:13.000
In that moment he had no idea there was a man called Zhuangzi.

113
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.330
Then he woke, and he was that man again.

114
00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:23.660
But now he did not know: had Zhuangzi dreamt he was a butterfly, or was a butterfly now dreaming it was Zhuangzi?

115
00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:28.270
The real force of this little story lies in its refusal to look for a solution.

116
00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:33.680
Descartes was looking for a way out; al-Ghazali was looking for salvation.

117
00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:37.650
Zhuangzi leaves the symmetry exactly as it stands.

118
00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:44.340
For him, the error is in stamping one of the two states with this is the real me and declaring the other false.

119
00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:49.110
Because the fixed I that would do the stamping is itself part of the problem.

120
00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:59.960
Elsewhere he speaks of people who weep on waking because they dreamt of a feast, and of people who go out hunting on waking because they wept in their sleep, and he adds this: people imagine they are awake.

121
00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:04.570
What Zhuangzi does is not to close the question but to learn to live with it.

122
00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:10.460
The answer Eastern thought gives to this matter is usually not a proof, but a stance.

123
00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:17.470
But at another corner of that same part of the world, there was someone who looked at the question as if it were evidence itself.

124
00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:28.800
Ibn Sina was born near Bukhara in 980 and died in 1037, and in his monumental work, the Kitab al-Shifa, that is, the Book of Healing, in the section on the soul,

125
00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.090
he set up one of the cleanest thought experiments in the history of philosophy.

126
00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:40.580
He puts it like this: imagine that a human being were created at this very moment, fully grown, in empty space.

127
00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:42.790
Suspended in midair.

128
00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:50.920
The limbs do not touch one another, there is nothing to see, nothing to hear, nothing to touch, no past, not a single memory.

129
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.930
Such a being could know nothing.

130
00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.980
Nothing at all?

131
00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:00.110
Ibn Sina's answer: it would know its own existence.

132
00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:07.120
It would not know its hand, it would not know its body, it would not know the world — but it would know the I.

133
00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:16.690
The purpose of this experiment in the Book of Healing was not to answer the doubt raised by dreaming; Ibn Sina was trying to show that the soul is not the same thing as the body.

134
00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:23.700
The method, even so, is strikingly the same: strip everything away, and look at what cannot be stripped.

135
00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:33.110
And the thing that cannot be stripped away lands, startlingly, in the same place as what would be discovered in Europe 6 centuries later: the existence of the subject itself.

136
00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:43.160
This is not identical to Descartes's sentence, and it would be unfair to claim the two are saying exactly the same thing — but on the question of what could possibly survive the scythe of doubt,

137
00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:46.490
two traditions arrived at a similar place.

138
00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.460
It is worth saying, too, how thin that foothold is.

139
00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:55.390
The man suspended in the air is certain of his own existence, and that is all.

140
00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.560
He does not know that the room is real.

141
00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:06.690
He does not know that the person sitting at the table across from him, whose voice he recognizes, with whom he has lived for years, is real.

142
00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:14.180
So this solid point is not a foundation on which a life can be built; it is a ledge grabbed by someone going under.

143
00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:19.110
Descartes filled the gap between them with the idea of a God who would not deceive him.

144
00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:23.080
Al-Ghazali filled it with a light descending into the heart.

145
00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:26.250
Zhuangzi refused to fill it at all.

146
00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:31.820
And for centuries this entire argument remained a cool-headed thought experiment.

147
00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:36.910
Because to have someone command all of your senses at once, you really did need a demon.

148
00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:39.120
Now a laptop will do.

149
00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.370
The sentence I saw it with my own eyes ends the argument in almost every language.

150
00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:49.940
Not hearsay, not secondhand, not interpretation; I saw it.

151
00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:54.230
The whole architecture of human certainty rests on that sentence.

152
00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:59.960
The witness in court, the journalist's recording, the family's oldest photograph, all of it.

153
00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:12.890
In January 2024, in Hong Kong, a finance officer working in the local office of the British engineering firm Arup received a message from the chief financial officer in London about a confidential transaction.

154
00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:17.340
The man's first reaction was the right one: this could be a scam.

155
00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:19.710
Then he joined a video call.

156
00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:30.880
Across from him was the chief financial officer, along with several other familiar colleagues; their faces were their own faces, their voices their own voices, their manners of speaking their own.

157
00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.210
After the meeting, he had no hesitation left.

158
00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:43.460
He sent a total of 200 million Hong Kong dollars, roughly 25 million US dollars, to 5 separate accounts across 15 transactions.

159
00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:52.550
When Hong Kong police announced the case in February 2024, they said this: the only real person in that meeting was the person being defrauded.

160
00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:58.760
Everyone else was a synthetic image generated from publicly available video and audio recordings.

161
00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:02.730
Arup confirmed in May 2024 that it was the victim.

162
00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:06.220
The real lesson of this story is not carelessness.

163
00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.910
That officer applied the test.

164
00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:13.360
He looked at the faces, he listened to the voices, he recognized them.

165
00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.050
The test simply no longer worked.

166
00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:25.460
The second and more insidious effect was given a name in a paper written in 2019 by the law professors Robert Chesney and Danielle Citron: the liar's dividend.

167
00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:31.030
As the fake becomes convincing, the genuine recording loses its force too.

168
00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:35.800
Anyone caught on camera now has a ready answer: that was generated.

169
00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:42.170
So the aim of the new deception is not to make you believe a lie; it is to leave you unable to believe anything.

170
00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:45.660
This is the cheap, realistic version of Descartes's demon.

171
00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:52.350
It does not need to command all of your senses; it is enough to command the channel through which you learn about the world.

172
00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:55.200
So what is any of this good for?

173
00:16:55.200 --> 00:17:03.810
Here is where the real residue of a 400-year-old argument, the part that is useful today, comes into view, and it is not individual certainty.

174
00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:07.780
Remember where the dream was weak: it was not in vividness.

175
00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:13.510
A dream is vivid, it is persuasive, it is emotionally stronger than waking life.

176
00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.520
Where the dream fails is consistency.

177
00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:24.170
The writing changes on second glance, clocks talk nonsense, the staircase you climbed does not bring you back to where you climbed from.

178
00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:32.780
And this is exactly the method lucid dreamers use: they do not look at a beautiful landscape and say that it looks so real; they look at a piece of writing twice.

179
00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:39.630
Because an illusion holds up to a single glance; it does not hold up to being tugged at from several directions.

180
00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:47.440
So the right question was never whether something looks real; it was whether it holds together when you pull at it from several directions at once.

181
00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:58.130
What a newsroom calls confirmation, what a court calls mutually corroborating evidence, what science calls independent replication, all of it is the same move: for a lie to stay standing,

182
00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:04.180
many unconnected threads have to have been woven at the same time, and woven so that they agree with one another.

183
00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:12.470
That is also the logic behind the work being done today on standards that would have cameras embed provenance information into an image at the very moment of capture:

184
00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:16.600
the file carrying the record of where it came from on its own back.

185
00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.130
None of this delivers certainty.

186
00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:24.060
All of it does the same thing: it raises the cost of forgery.

187
00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:31.710
And in the end, this is what remains: wakefulness is not a condition you test once and then pocket, but a labour you keep up.

188
00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:37.120
The man who gave this answer most plainly was the one who paid the steepest price for the question.

189
00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:46.370
Years later, in 1106, al-Ghazali began teaching again in Nishapur, and he did not do it because he was holding some proof that refuted doubt.

190
00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:50.180
He had written that no such proof could ever be found.

191
00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:59.750
Even so, he returned to living, to teaching, to passing judgment; because the answer to doubt was not to be found in doubt's own language, but inside a life.

192
00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:05.000
Descartes left his demon behind in the first meditation and spent the rest of his life building.

193
00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:09.130
Zhuangzi kept the question open and did not chase the butterfly.

194
00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:17.100
And Ibn Sina's man suspended in the air is still there: not touching, not seeing, not remembering, yet existing.

195
00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:21.870
The person inside the dream never asks whether they are awake.

196
00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:27.280
Asking that question is not proof of wakefulness — but it is wakefulness's only habit.

197
00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:31.250
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

198
00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:35.060
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
